/	BIGCON FOR EARLY P?S/8

/	LAST EDIT: 23-MAR-2013	CJL

/	ORIGINALLY WRITTEN BY RS AND CJL CIRCA 1975 AS A PRECURSOR TO A MORE ADVANCED
/	VERSION OF CONVRT THAT INCLUDES ADDITIONAL OPERATING MODES [WRITTEN BY CJL].

/	THIS WAS A QUICK-AND-DIRTY STOPGAP PROGRAM USED TO CONVERT OS/8 SOURCE FILES
/	TO A FORMAT ACCESSIBLE BY P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS SUCH AS P?S/8 PAL.  IT MAY BE
/	MADE MORE ROBUST FOR USE ON EXISTENT P?S/8 TAPES THAT PREDATE THE REDESIGNED
/	CONVRT PROGRAM THAT MADE THIS CODE OBSOLETE.

/	DISASSEMBLED BY CJL 10-DEC-2012 THROUGH 23-MAR-2013 INTERMITTENTLY.

/	SYSTEM DEFINITIONS.

	SBOOT=	7600			/SYSTEM RESTART ADDRESS
	SEQUALS=7756			/SYSTEM EQUALS PARAMETER ADDRESS
	SFILES=	7757			/PASSED FILE LIST STARTS HERE
	SOUTFLS=7607			/PASSED OUTPUT FILE COUNT
	SWAL=	7604			/PASSED OPTION SWITCHES /A-/L
	SWMX=	7605			/PASSED OPTION SWITCHES /M-/X
	SWY9=	7606			/PASSED OPTION SWITCHES /Y-/9
	SYSIO=	7640			/SYSTEM HANDLER ENTRY POINT
	WRITE=	4000			/SYSTEM HANDLER WRITE FUNCTION BIT

/	NUMERIC LOAD DEFINITIONS.

	NL0002=	CLA CLL CML RTL		/LOAD AC WITH 0002
	NL2000=	CLA CLL CML RTR		/LOAD AC WITH 2000
	NL7775=	CLA CLL CMA RTL		/LOAD AC WITH 7775
	NL7776=	CLA CLL CMA RAL		/LOAD AC WITH 7776
	NL7777=	CLA CMA			/LOAD AC WITH 7777

/	ADDITIONAL DEFINITIONS.

	CNTLZP=	"Z&37!200		/<^Z> WITH MARK PARITY SET
	DEFBLK=	16			/DEFAULT OS/8 RECORD IN BLOCKS
	PFILBUF=3000			/P?S/8 FORMAT OUTPUT BUFFER STARTING ADDRESS
	PQSGETC=0200			/OPERATING ADDRESS FOR P?S/8 GETCHAR ROUTINE
	PQIBUFF=4400			/P?S/8 FORMAT INPUT BUFFER STARTING ADDRESS
	PQISIZE=15			/P?S/8 FORMAT INPUT BUFFER SIZE
	OGETCHR=7000			/OPERATING ADDRESS FOR OS/8 GETCHAR ROUTINE
	OSIBUFF=0200			/OS/8 FORMAT INPUT BUFFER STARTING ADDRESS
	OSISIZE=13			/OS/8 FORMAT INPUT BUFFER SIZE
	OOUTBUF=0377			/OS/8 FORMAT OUTPUT BUFFER STARTING ADDRESS
	OS8SIZE=20			/OS/8 FORMAT OUTPUT BUFFER SIZE
	ODEFUNI=7			/OS/8 DEFAULT UNIT IF /0-/7 NOT USED

/	NOTE: MAY BE ASSEMBLED WITH /J [OS/8 PAL8 /F] SWITCH SET.
/	PROGRAM USAGE.

/	P?S/8 TO OS/8 CONVERSION.

/	.R BIGCON INFIL1,INFIL2	[ADDITIONAL INPUT FILES AS NECESSARY] [OPTIONS]

/	IN THIS MODE, THE CONTENTS OF P?S/8 TEXT INPUT FILES ARE BEING CONVERTED TO A
/	SINGLE OS/8 OUTPUT FILE ON A DEVICE AND BLOCK NUMBER DETERMINED BY A
/	COMBINATION OF DEFAULT AND COMMAND OPTIONS.  NOTE: IN THIS DIRECTION OF FILE
/	CONVERSION, THE PROGRAM OPERATES IDENTICALLY TO THE CONVRT PROGRAM.  FILES
/	THAT CANNOT BE EDITED WITH THE P?S/8 KEYBOARD MONITOR/EDITOR AS WELL AS
/	EDITABLE TINY FILE SYSTEM (TFS) FILES ARE VALID AS INPUT FILES TO BE CONVERTED
/	TO OS/8 FORMAT OUTPUT.

/	NOTE: THIS PROGRAM [AND ALSO THE CONVRT PROGRAM] DOES NOT PERFORM ANY OS/8
/	DIRECTORY OPERATIONS.  OS/8 UTILITIES EXIST TO FLESH OUT THE DIRECTORY AFTER
/	P?S/8 TEXT IS CONVERTED TO OS/8 FORMAT ON DESIGNATED OS/8 RECORDS.  SEE
/	DOCUMENTATION OF OS/8 PIP FOR INFORMATION ON CREATING OS/8 DIRECTORY ENTRIES
/	AS REQUIRED.

/	NOTE: FUTURE CONVERSION UTILITIES THAT UTILIZE P?S/8 NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS COULD
/	PERFORM OS/8 DIRECTORY OPERATIONS SINCE THE SCOPE OF STORAGE HANDLED BY THESE
/	HANDLERS MEETS OR EXCEEDS OS/8'S CAPABILITIES.

/	OS/8 TO P?S/8 CONVERSION.

/	.R BIGCON OUTFL1, OUTFL2 [ADDITIONAL OUTPUT FILES AS NECESSARY] < [OPTIONS]

/	IN THIS MODE, THE CONTENTS OF A SINGLE OS/8 INPUT FILE ARE BEING CONVERTED TO
/	ONE OR MORE P?S/8 OUTPUT FILES IN A TEXT FORMAT SUITABLE FOR INPUT TO P?S/8
	SYSTEM PROGRAMS.  uNLIKE THE ORIGINAL CONVRT PROGRAM, FILES PRODUCED BY BIGCON ARE
/	NOT INTENDED TO BE EDITED FURTHER IN THE P?S/8 ENVIRONMENT AS CREATED.

/	LOGISTIC CONSIDERATIONS OF P?S/8 FILE CONVERSION.

/	******************************************************************************

/	THE P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER IS USED WHEN ACCESSING OS/8 TEXT DATA FOR ALL
/	CONVERSION PURPOSES.  AS SUCH, THE OS/8 DEVICE MUST BE COMPATIBLE WITH THE
/	P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER IN TERMS OF INTRINSIC DATA FORMAT, WHICH IS GENERALLY THE
/	CASE.  EXAMPLES INCLUDE DTA1: ETC. ON OS/8 TD8E OR TC01/08 DECTAPE WHICH READ
/	AND WRITE THE UNDERLYING DATA IDENTICALLY IN BOTH OPERATING SYSTEMS.  THERE
/	ARE OTHER DEVICES THAT READ OR WRITE DATA IDENTICALLY IN BOTH SYSTEMS SUCH AS
/	LINCTAPE FOR THE PDP-12 AND MODIFIED 8K LINC-8, RX01/02, RK83/RK05, 256 BYTE
/	MDC8 SCSI CONTROLLER AND DRIVES, DSD-240 DISK DRIVE.  THE ONLY REQUIREMENT IS
/	HANDLERS FROM BOTH OPERATING SYSTEMS ADDRESS THE UNDERLYING STORAGE IN THE
/	SAME MANNER FOR ALL READ/WRITE PURPOSES.  FOR COMPATIBLE DEVICES, THERE IS A
/	LOGICAL BLOCK ARRANGEMENT THAT MUST BE FOLLOWED:

/	P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLERS ADDRESS 128 12-BIT WORD LOGICAL BLOCKS 0000 THROUGH AN
/	UPPER LIMIT THAT CAN BE AS HIGH AS 7777 ON MAXIMUM SIZE P?S/8 DEVICES.  IN
/	SUCH A SITUATION, THIS WOULD BE EXACTLY THE FIRST HALF OF WHAT A CORRESPONDING
/	OS/8 HANDLER OF MAXIMUM SIZE CAN ADDRESS.  FOR SMALLER DEVICES SUCH AS DECTAPE
/	AND LINCTAPE, THE DEVICE IS FAR SMALLER THAN THIS THEORETICAL LIMIT.  STANDARD
/	DECTAPES CAN BE AS LARGE AS BLOCKS 0000 THROUGH 2701.  ON THE RK8E/RK05, EACH
/	LOGICAL UNIT OF THE P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER ADDRESSES BLOCK 0000 THROUGH 6257
/	WHICH MAPS IDENTICALLY TO THE CORRESPONDING OS/8 DEVICE'S FIRST HALF.  OS/8
/	RK05/RK8E DEVICES ADDRESS TWICE THIS SPACE PRODUCING THE USUAL COINCIDENT
/	NUMBERS:  THE NUMBER OF 128 WORD BLOCKS A P?S/8 LOGICAL UNIT CAN HANDLER IS
/	THE SAME COUNT AS THE NUMBER OF 256 WORD RECORDS OS/8 CAN ADDRESS ON THE
/	SAME DEVICE HARDWARE.

  LARGER DEVICES SUPPORT
/	LARGER STILL LARGER BLOCK RANGES, UP TO 0000-THROUGH 7777 ON LARGE MAPPED HARD DISKS
/	SUCH AS THE DSD-240.

/	DEVICES THAT CAN BE READ OR WRITEN BY THE P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER FOR THE SAME
/	HARDWARE.  OTHER KNOWN COINCIDENT HANDLER-COMPATIBLE DEVICES INCLUDE LINCTAPE
/	FOR THE PDP-12 OR 8K MODIFIED LINC-8, RX01/02, RK8E/RK05 AND OTHER DEVICES
/	WHERE THE DATA LAYOUT OF THE TWO SYSTEMS SUCH THAT A P?S/8 LOGICAL UNIT MAPS
/	INTO THE IDENTICAL UNDERLYING STORAGE OF A CORRESPONDING OS/8 DEVICE. SINCE
/	THE P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER ONLY ACCESSES AT MOST ONE HALF OF THE MAXIMUM SIZE OF
/	AN OS/8 DEVICE, THIS DEFINES THE LOGICAL BLOCKS THE CONVERSION DATA MUST
/	COMPLETELY RESIDE IN.  FOR MOST OF THE ACTUAL HARDWARE DEVICES USED, THIS IS
/	NOT A CONSIDERATION OTHER THAN ON THE LARGER HARD DISKS; ON THESE DEVICES, IT
/	MAY OCCASIONALLY BE NECESSARY TO CAREFULLY POSITION FILES FOR PROPER
/	CONVERSION; OCCASIONALLY, IT MAY BE NECESSARY TO SPLIT THE OS/8 DATA INTO TWO
/	PARTS AS WELL AS ENSURING THE PORTION BEING CONVERTED IS TOTALLY RESIDING IN
/	SUFFICIENTLY LOW OS/8 RECORDS TO ALLOW THE CONVERSION.

/	NOTE: FUTURE VERSIONS OF CONVERSION PROGRAMS MAY USE P?S/8 NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS
/	WHICH ADDRESS FAR LARGER DEVICES THAN OS/8.  HOWEVER, THIS HAS NOT BEEN
/	IMPLEMENTED AS OF THE TIME OF THE LAST EDIT OF THIS SOURCE FILE; THE MAIN
/	PURPOSE OF THIS FILE IS TO ILLUSTRATE THE ORIGINAL IMPLEMENTATION OF THE
/	CONVERSION FROM OS/8 TO LARGER P?S/8 FILES SUITABLE FOR INPUT TO P?S/8 SYSTEM
/	PROGRAMS SUCH AS P?S/8 PAL AS OPPOSED TO MAJOR REFINEMENTS PERFORMED OVER A
/	TIME PERIOD MEASURED IN DECADES.





/	PRIOR TO CONVERSION TO 

/	[NOTE: BY CAREFUL CONFIGURATION OF THE OPTION SWITCHES WHEN USING THE PROGRAM,
/	IT IS POSSIBLE TO CONTROL THE EXACT SIZE OF ANY ONE OF THESE FILES
/	SUFFICIENTLY TO ALLOW INDEPENDENT CONVERSION BACK TO OS/8 FORMAT USING EITHER
/	BIGCON OR CONVRT FOLLOWED BY USING CONVRT TO CREATE A GROUPING OF COMPATIBLE
/	TEXT FILES THAT CAN BE EDITED BY THE KEYBOARD MONITOR/EDITOR ENVIRONMENT.
/	HOWEVER, THE PRIMARY PURPOSE OF BIGCON IS TO CREATE AS MANY AS EIGHT OUTPUT
/	FILES THAT AGGREGATE TO THE CONVERTED EQUIVALENT OF THE ORIGINAL OS/8 FILE.
/	SINCE EACH LOGICAL UNIT IN A FULLY-IMPLEMENTED P?S/8 SYSTEM CAN HOLD ONE OF
/	THESE FILES, IF P?S/8 TEXT EDITING IS CONTEMPLATED, THIS MEANS THAT ONE
/	SOURCE PROGRAM CAN BE THE EQUIVALENT OF UP TO 17 P?S/8 TINY FILE SYSTEM [TFS]
/	FILES PER LOGICAL UNIT.  THUS THE TOTAL SIZE OF INPUT FILES IN A PROPERLY
/	CONFIGURED PROJECT CAN BE THE EQUIVALENT OF 136 2K-LONG TFS FILES.  IF THERE
/	IS NO INTENTION OF EDITING THESE FILES IN THE P?S/8 EDITING ENVIRONMENT,
/	LONGER FILES CAN BE CREATED LIMITED ONLY BY THE PHYSICAL SIZE OF EACH DEVICE.
/	HOWEVER, THERE ARE FEW AND FAR BETWEEN ANY ACTUAL PAL LANGUAGE PROJECTS THAT
/	EVEN APPROACH THIS UPPER LIMIT.  ASSUMING A TYPICAL STARTING BLOCK OF AROUND
/	1000 OCTAL AND MAXIMUM SIZE P?S/8 LOGICAL UNITS, SUCH AS WOULD BE THE TYPICAL
/	CASE ON A LARGE IMPLEMENTATION OF P?S/8 SUCH AS ON A MAPPED PORTION OF A
/	LARGE DISK, THIS WOULD ALLOW 7000 OCTAL BLOCKS PER OUTPUT FILE IF EDITING IS
/	NOT REQUIRED.  ASSUMING ALL 8 LOGICAL UNITS ARE AVAILABLE FOR THE CONVERSION
/	PROCESS, THIS WOULD ALLOW THE AGGREGATE SIZE OF THE CONVERTED OS/8 SOURCE
/	INPUT FILE TO BE NEARLY 1800 2K-LONG TFS FILES, ALBEIT NOT EDITABLE IN THE
/	KEYBOARD MONITOR/EDITOR ENVIRONMENT.  HOWEVER, THIS AGGREGATE SIZE IS LARGER
/	THAN THE LARGEST OS/8 FILE SIZE POSSIBLE.  ADDITIONALLY, SINCE THE P?S/8
/	SYSTEM HANDLER IS USED TO ACCESS THE OS/8 DATA, THE MAXIMUM OS/8 FILE SIZE
/	ALLOWED IS SLIGHTLY UNDER HALF OF THE MAXIMUM ALLOWED OS/8 SIZE, LARGELY
/	MAKING THIS UPPER LIMIT MOOT.  AS NECESSARY, THE OS/8 INPUT DATA MAY HAVE
/	TO BE SPLIT INTO TWO PORTIONS EACH SUFFICIENTLY SMALL TO MEET THE REQUIREMENT
/	THAT THE ENTIRE FILE BE ACCESSIBLE FROM THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  AS
/	NECESSARY, THE RESULTANT P?S/8 OUTPUT FILES CAN BE LOGICALLY CONCATENATED
/	TOGETHER AS ORIGINALLY INTENDED IN THE OS/8 ENVIRONMENT.  IT IS DOUBTFUL IF
/	AS MANY AS SIX PAL PROJECTS IN HISTORY WERE EVER WRITTEN TO EXCEED THIS
/	LIMITATION.  IN POINT OF FACT, NEARLY ALL FILES EVER WRITTEN IN PAL CAN FIT
/	ON A SINGLE DECTAPE IN SOURCE FORM, AND THE RESTRICTION LIMITS ANY ONE OS/8
/	FILE TO WHAT IN FACT FITS ON THE EQUIVALENT OF TWO OS/8 DECTAPES.]

/	
 CONVRT ONE OF THESE FILES INDIRECTLY INTO A GROUP OF TEXT
/	FILES COMPATIBLE  
 THE CREATED OUTPUT FILES ARE NOT
/	COMPATIBLE WITH THE P?S/8 KEYBOARD MONITOR'S BUILT-IN TEXT EDITOR AND ARE THUS
P?S/8 TEXT MODE FORMAT SUITABLE FOR INPUT TO OTHER SYSTEM PROGRAMS.  UNLIKE
/	THE CONVRT PROGRAM, BIGCON DOES NOT CREATE FILES USABLE WITH THE KEYBOARD
/	MONITOR'S BUILT-IN EDITOR.  HOWEVER, THIS DOES ALLOW APPLICATIONS SUCH AS
/	ASSEMBLING VERY LONG SOURCE CODE PROGRAMS THAT CANNOT BE EXPRESSED IN 17 OR
/	LESS FIXED-LENGTH TEXT FILES WITH LINE NUMBERS, ETC.  PROJECTS OF THAT SIZE
/	WERE NEVER INTENDED TO BE USED IN THE BASIC MODE OF P?S/8.  VARIOUS EXTENSIONS
/	TO P?S/8 HAVE BEEN DESIGNED TO OVERCOME THIS LIMITATION AND ARE IN A VARIETY OF
/	STATES OF IMPLEMENTATION.  IT IS HOPED THE EVENTUAL IMPLEMENTATION OF THE P?S/8
/	SHELL WILL PRESENT A SOLUTION THAT DOES NOT DEPEND ON OS/8 OR ANOTHER EXTERNAL
/	SYSTEM IN ANY WAY [WHICH IN TURN WILL ALSO ELIMINATE DEPENDENCY ON WEAKNESSES
/	OF THESE OTHER SYSTEMS].

/	OPTIONS FOR BIGCON USAGE.

/	ADDITIONAL COMMAND SWITCHES WILL BE ENUMERATED HERE.

/	/0 THROUGH /7 IS THE LOGICAL UNIT FOR THE OS/8 MEDIA DRIVE.  THE ACTUAL INPUT
/	OR OUTPUT IS PERFORMED BY THE P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER WITH THIS LOGICAL
/	UNIT VALUE PASSED. IF NONE OF THESE SWITCHES ARE PRESENT IN THE COMMAND LINE
/	THE DEFAULT UNIT IS 7 AS THE PROGRAM WAS ORIGINALLY RELEASED. [NOTE: THIS CAN
/	BE CHANGED BY MODIFYING THE SOURCE CODE.]

/	=XXXX IS THE STARTING RECORD NUMBER FOR THE OS/8 MEDIA DRIVE.  VALUES ARE
/	RESTRICTED TO UNDER 4000 OCTAL [2048 DECIMAL] DUE TO THE RESTRICTIONS OF THE
/	P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER; THE ENTIRETY OF THE OS/8 FILE TO BE CONVERTED
/	MUST EXIST ON RECORDS LOWER THAN 4000 OCTAL.  IF THE =XXXX CONSTRUCT IS NOT
/	PRESENT IN THE COMMAND LINE, THE DEFAULT VALUE OF 0007 WILL BE USED. [NOTE:
/	OS/8 RECORD 0007 IS THE VALUE USED BY OS/8 FOR THE FIRST FILE ON AN OTHERWISE
/	EMPTY DEVICE.]
/	INTERNAL MEMORY LAYOUT.

/	BIGCON IS NOT ASSEMBLED AS A SIMPLE LOADABLE PROGRAM.  THIS ALLOWS BETTER
/	PERFORMANCE ON 4K SYSTEMS BIGCON IS DESIGNED TO RUN ON.  IT DOES NOT TAKE ANY
/	ADVANTAGE OF ADDITIONAL MEMORY THAT MAY BE PRESENT.  BY USING INTERNAL
/	SELF-RELOCATION, THE PROGRAM REORGANIZES ITSELF AS A FUNCTION OF THE OPERATING
/	MODE [TO OR FROM OS/8 FORMAT DATA].

/	WHEN RUNNING IN OS/8 OUTPUT MODE, THE OS/8 FORMAT BUFFER IS 00377-04376 AND
/	WRITTEN 20 BLOCKS AT A TIME. [NOTE: IN THEORY, THIS ALSO COULD HAVE BEEN
/	00400-04377.  HOWEVER, THE LOW-ORDER ADDRESS BIT FIGURES INTO THE PROGRAM
/	LOGIC THUS THE VALUE MUST NOT BE CHANGED UNLESS THE CORRESPONDING CODING IS
/	ADJUSTED ACCORDINGLY.] P?S/8 FORMAT IS READ INTO 04400-07577 AND READ 15
/	BLOCKS AT A TIME.  SINCE P?S/8 LINE NUMBERS ARE IGNORED, THE HIGHEST ADDRESS
/	CONTENTS OF ANY PARTICULAR FILE ARE GENERALLY NOT NEEDED; THE INPUT WILL BE READ
/	INTO THE BUFFER AS NECESSARY.  EACH FILE ENDS WITH A SENTINEL WORD OF 0000 AT WHICH
/	POINT ANY ADDITIONAL FILES ARE PROCESSED.  EXTENDED LENGTH P?S/8 FILES WILL
/	GENERALLY REQUIRE MULTIPLE READS TO PROCESS ALL INPUT.

/	THE RUNNING PROGRAM THAT ACCOMPLISHES THIS IS LOCATED IN PAGE ZERO [00000-00177]
/	AND PAGE 1 [00200-00377] THROUGH LOCATION 00376.  THE CODE FOR PAGE 1 IS INITIALLY
/	LOADED INTO 00600 ONWARD AND IS THEN SOFTWARE RELOCATED TO 00200 ONWARD BY ONCE-ONLY
/	CODE LOADED INTO 01000-01177 WHERE THE PROGRAM STARTS UP TO PROCESS COMMAND OPTIONS,
/	ETC.  THE ONCE-ONLY CODE MAKES THE DECISION TO OPERATE IN THIS MODE AFTER
/	DETERMINING THERE ARE PASSED P?S/8 INPUT FILE PARAMETERS AND THE COUNT OF
/	OUTPUT FILES IS ZERO.

/	AN EXPLANATION FOR THE OS/8 INPUT MODE NEEDS TO BE ADDED HERE.



/	DESIGN CONSIDERATIONS.

/	WHILE THIS IS THE EARLIEST VERSION OF A BI-DIRECTIONAL TEXT CONVERSION PROGRAM
/	FOR P?S/8 WITHOUT LINE NUMBERS, THERE WERE SEVERAL DESIGN ISSUES BEING
/	ADDRESSED FROM THE BEGINNING.  WHEN ONLY TINY FILE SYSTEM [TFS] FILES ARE TO
/	BE CONSIDERED, IT IS NEVER PRODUCTIVE TO READ IN THE ENTIRE FILE AT ONCE.
/	THIS IS BECAUSE PREDICTABLY THE END OF THE 2K FILE IS LINE START AND LINE
/	NUMBER INFORMATION AND NOT TEXT.  THE MORE LINES PRESENT, THE MORE SPACE IS
/	TAKEN UP BY THIS NON-TEXTUAL DATA THAT IS NOT NEEDED WHEN READING P?S/8 TEXT
/	FILES FOR CONVERSION TO OS/8.  THUS THERE IS A PRACTICAL UPPER LIMIT OF AROUND
/	15 BLOCKS FOR THE TYPICAL FULL TFS FILE. [FILES THAT AREN'T FULL PROVIDE EVEN
/	LESS TEXT PER BUFFER.] AS SUCH, 15 PAGES WERE CHOSEN AS THE PRACTICAL SIZE FOR
/	A P?S/8 INPUT BUFFER.  THIS IN TURN ALLOWS FOR A LARGER OS/8 OUTPUT BUFFER
/	WHICH INCREASES OVERALL THROUGHPUT WHEN CREATING OS/8 TEXT OUTPUT, ETC.

/	LATER VERSIONS OF PROGRAMS FOR P?S/8 INCLUDE EXTENDED MEMORY CONSIDERATIONS
/	WHICH CAN CHANGE THE DESIGN RULES DYNAMICALLY AS A FUNCTION OF AVAILABLE
/	MEMORY.  IF SUFFICIENT MEMORY IS AVAILABLE, AS MUCH AS 40 BLOCKS CAN BE USED
/	FOR BOTH AN INPUT BUFFER AND AN OUTPUT BUFFER, ETC.  HOWEVER, THIS PROGRAM AS
/	WRITTEN PREDATES THE GENERAL OPTIMIZATION OF PERFORMANCE WITH RESPECT TO USING MORE
/	AVAILABLE MEMORY.  DESPITE THIS, PERFORMANCE SHOULD BE AT LEAST 50 PERCENT OF THE
/	THEORETICAL MAXIMUM PERFORMANCE ON LARGER MACHINE UTILITIZING ONLY 4K, ETC.

/	WRITING LARGER OS/8 OUTPUT BUFFERS IS OBVIOUSLY SUPERIOR TO SMALLER ONES.  IN
/	THE CASE OF TFS FILES IT IS CLEAR THAT THERE IS NO POSSIBLE ADVANTAGE TO
/	HAVING A LARGER P?S/8 INPUT BUFFER DUE TO THE MAXIMUM FILE SIZE OF 20 BLOCKS.
/	HOWEVER, WITH UTILITIES SUCH AS BIGCON AND FOLLOW-ON DESCENDANT PROGRAMS SUCH
/	AS THE MORE MODERN VERSION OF CONVRT, THE P?S/8 INPUT COULD BE THE CONTENTS OF
/	A MUCH LARGER TEXT FILE WITHOUT LINE NUMBERS.  IN THAT CASE, THE LARGER THE
/	INPUT BUFFER THE BETTER, ETC.  HOWEVER, THIS EARLY PROGRAM DOES NOT USE
/	EXTENDED MEMORY AT ALL.  IT WAS JUST AN ATTEMPT TO DO THE BEST WITHIN A STRICTLY 4K
/	ENVIRONMENT, ETC.

/	VARIOUS LESSER ISSUES FOUND IN THIS CODE WERE CHANGED IN LATER VERSIONS.
/	ALTHOUGH TECHNICALLY NOT A REQUIREMENT GIVEN THE WAY P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLERS
/	FUNCTION, THE OS/8 WRITE BUFFER WAS ALIGNED ON OS/8 RECORD BOUNDARIES. [NOTE:
/	ONE OF THE AUTHORS OF THIS EARLY PROGRAM WAS NOT FULLY FAMILIAR WITH A
/	FUNDAMENTAL DESIGN DIFFERENCE BETWEEN OS/8 AND P?S/8; THE DESIGN REASONING WAS
/	THUS "CONTAMINATED" BY IMPROPERLY USING OS/8-SPECIFIC DESIGN CONSIDERATIONS
/	WHERE THEY DIDN'T APPLY.  OS/8 DEVICE HANDLERS ARE NOT CAPABLE OF ACCESSING
/	THE SECOND "HALF" OF A LOGICAL DOUBLE-PAGE SIZE RECORD.  READ AND WRITE
/	OPERATIONS ARE ALLOWED TO ACCESS THE FIRST HALF OF A RECORD ONLY, BUT THE
/	SECOND HALF MUST BE INCLUDED WITH THE FIRST HALF, I.E., IT IS NECESSARY TO
/	HAVE A TWO-PAGE BUFFER FOR ALL PURPOSES WHERE THE SECOND HALF OF THE RECORD IS
/	REQUIRED.  FURTHER, WRITING AN OS/8 RECORD FIRST HALF ONLY CAUSES UNCERTAINTY
/	WITH REGARD TO THE SECOND HALF OF THE RECORD; THE ONLY REQUIREMENT IS THAT
/	READING BOTH HALVES LATER CAN BE ACCOMPLISHED WITHOUT ERROR.  SECOND HALVES OF
/	RECORDS COULD EITHER BE THE FORMER CONTENTS, OR SOMETHING QUITE DIFFERENT
/	RANGING FROM BUFFER JUNK TO ALL ZEROS DATA DEPENDING ON THE EXACT HARDWARE.]
/	P?S/8 HANDLERS ADDRESS DEVICES AS 128 WORD BLOCKS FOR ALL READ AND WRITE
/	OPERATIONS.  THERE ARE NO RESTRICTIONS DEALING WITH THE SECOND HALF DATA OF
/	OS/8 LOGICAL RECORDS ORGANIZED AS DOUBLE-PAGE RECORDS, ETC.  IF A P?S/8 READ
/	OR WRITE CALL ADDRESSED ONLY THE FIRST HALF OF THE RECORD, A SUBSEQUENT
/	OPERATION CAN ACCESS THE SECOND HALF WITHOUT LOSS.  THERE SIMPLY IS NO MERIT
/	TO RESTRICT OS/8 OPERATIONS TO DOUBLE-PAGES WHEN P?S/8 HANDLERS ARE PERFORMING
/	THE READ OR WRITE OPERATIONS.  HAD THIS BEEN KNOWN AT THE TIME OF CREATING
/	THIS EARLY PROGRAM, IT IS POSSIBLE IT WOULD HAVE OPERATED SLIGHTLY DIFFERENTLY
/	BY THE TIME IT WAS RELEASED.  THERE ARE CERTAIN CODE COMPLICATIONS THAT STEM
/	FROM THIS ERRANT DECISION; THE CODE FUNCTIONS PER SE, JUST THAT IT IS LIKELY
/	IT WOULD HAVE TURNED OUT AS A SOMEWHAT BETTER PRODUCT AT THE TIME.  [THE NEWER
/	PROGRAMS DO NOT HAVE ANY OF THESE LIMITATIONS; THEY WERE ALL WRITTEN TOTALLY
/	BY CJL WHO HAS ALWAYS FULLY UNDERSTOOD THESE ISSUES, ETC.]

/	THE ONLY ACTUAL ADVANTAGE OS/8 HANDLERS HAVE IS THE ABILITY TO ACCESS TWICE AS
/	MANY BLOCKS AS THE LARGEST P?S/8 HANDLER FOR ANY LOGICAL DEVICE UNIT.  AS
/	SUCH, THE ENTIRETY OF THE OS/8 DATA MUST BE CONTAINED WITHIN OS/8 RECORDS LESS
/	THAN 4000 [0000-7777 P?S/8 BLOCKS PER DEVICE].

/	THIS RESTRICTION REMAINS TO THIS DAY.  HOWEVER, ONE SINGLE OS/8 TEXT FILE AS
/	LONG AS 2048 RECORDS IS HIGHLY UNLIKELY.  SECTIONING THE DATA INTO SMALLER
/	CHUNKS IS NECESSARY IF THIS CANNOT BE ACHIEVED; P?S/8 CONVERSION CAN BE
/	CARRIED OUT ON EACH SUCH CHUNK, ETC.  ASSUMING A SUFFICIENTLY LARGE P?S/8
/	SYSTEM SUCH AS A LARGER HARD DISK MAPPED INTO AS MANY AS EIGHT MAXIMUM-SIZED
/	P?S/8 LOGICAL UNITS, THE TOTAL SIZE OF ANY ONE PROJECT CAN BE AS MUCH AS THE
/	ENTIRE CONTENTS OF 4 MAXIMUM SIZE OS/8 DEVICES.

/	TO ILLUSTRATE WHAT THIS MEANS, CONSIDER THAT ANY ONE OS/8 DEVICE HANDLER FOR
/	RK05 DISKS ADDRESSES EXACTLY ONE-HALF OF THE DISK, AND THAT EACH IS ABOUT 3/4
/	THE MAXIMUM SIZE OS/8 ALLOWS.  WITH PROPER TECHNIQUE, BIGCON OR ANALOGOUS
/	TEXT CONVERSION UTILITIES CAN PRODUCE UP TO EIGHT LARGE P?S/8 TEXT FILES, EACH
/	OF WHICH IS APPROXIMATELY 2/3 THE MAXIMUM SIZE OF ONE RK05 DEVICE SUCH AS
/	RKB0: ETC.  THAT WOULD ROUGHLY CORRESPOND TO TWO FULL RK05 PLATTERS AND
/	ANOTHER PARTIAL DEVICE IN TURN COVERED BY A TOTAL OF 5 OS/8 HANDLERS.  THUS,
/	EVEN IF THE SOURCE FILE FOR A PROJECT WERE GARGANTUAN IN SIZE, IT IS STILL
/	POSSIBLE TO BE SUCCESSFULLY CONVERTED TO A VIABLE P?S/8 ENVIRONMENT.  THERE
/	ARE ONLY TWO REQUIREMENTS: A) ANY ONE FILE FROM OS/8 CAN BE ADDRESSED IN
/	LOGICAL BLOCKS THAT ARE WITHIN THE FIRST HALF OR THE ENTIRETY OF THE MAXIMUM
/	SIZE IT COULD BE [WHERE AN RK05 LOGICAL DEVICE IS ONLY ABOUT 3/4 OF THAT
/	MAXIMUM; STILL SOME OF THE SPACE WOULD BE INACCESSIBLE TO THE P?S/8 SYSTEM
/	DEVICE HANDLER, BUT MOST OF THE STORAGE WOULD NOT BE A PROBLEM, B) THE ENTIRE
/	PROCESS MUST PRODUCE NO MORE THAN EIGHT TOTAL EXTRA-LONG FILES.  P?S/8 COMMAND
/	LINES ALLOW UP TO 17 FILES.  THIS WOULD ALLOW AT LEAST 11 BINARY OUTPUT FILES
/	ASSUMING THIS EXAMPLE COVERS SOME MYTHICAL MONSTROUS PAL PROJECT, ETC.  AS A
/	PRACTICAL MATTER, EVEN VERY LARGE ASSEMBLIES ARE FAR LESS THAN 1000 OS/8
/	LOGICAL RECORDS IN ANY ONE FILE.

/	ALSO KNOWN AT THE TIME OF STARTING TO DESIGN THIS SERIES OF PROGRAMS WAS THE
/	LIMITATIONS OF OS/8 WITH REGARD TO MEDIOCRE MEMORY MANAGEMENT THAT CREATES
/	RESTRICTIONS NOT FOUND IN P?S/8.  OS/8 SIMPLY HAS A RULE PREVENTING PROGRAM
/	ACCESS TO X7600-X7777 FOR ANY FIELD OF MEMORY X.  BY CONTRAST, P?S/8 ONLY
/	RESTRICTS ACCESS TO 07600-07777; THIS IS IN TURN BASED ON HARDWARE-BASED
/	RESTRICTIONS SUCH AS THE HARD-WIRED LOCATIONS FOR THREE-CYCLE DATA BREAKS AND
/	THE SOFTWARE CONVENTIONS THAT APPLIED DUE TO THE HARDWARE RESTRICTIONS.  WHILE
/	OS/8 GENERALIZED THE RESTRICTION IN AN OVERSIMPLIFIED WAY, IT IS ARGUABLE THAT
/	THE RESULTANT SYSTEM LEAVES A LOT TO BE DESIRED.

/	ONE CONSEQUENTIAL WEAKNESS IS THE ALLOCATION OF MEMORY BUFFERS FOR ANY OS/8
/	PROGRAM.  SINCE OS/8 REQUIRES DOUBLE-PAGE BUFFERS FOR ALL INPUT AND OUTPUT,
/	ALL CASES OF ODD PAGE COUNTS ARE NOT ALLOWED.  BECAUSE X7600-X7777 IS ALSO
/	NOT ALLOWED, THE LARGEST BUFFER BECOMES 36 (30 DECIMAL) PAGES DISALLOWING 40
/	(32 DECIMAL) PAGES WHEN DESIGNING FOR AN ENTIRE MEMORY FIELD.  [NOTE: THE OS/8
/	HANDLERS CAN ADDRESS THIS SPACE; BOTH SYSTEMS SUPPORT HANDLER CALLS FOR THE
/	SAME 32 CASES OF PAGE COUNTS 1 THROUGH 32 IN THE BUFFER.] THUS, OS/8 CAN ONLY
/	ALLOW A PROGRAM DESIGNER TO ALLOCATE JUST UNDER HALF THE CASES POSSIBLE IN
/	P?S/8 WHICH ALLOWS ANY NUMBER OF PAGES OF BUFFER 1 THROUGH 32 INCLUSIVE.  THIS
/	HANDICAPS BOTH THE SMALLEST BUFFER PROGRAMS THAT MIGHT FUNCTION WELL WITH A
/	ONE-PAGE BUFFER AS WELL AS MAKING PROGRAMS WANTING TO ALLOCATE THE LARGEST
/	POSSIBLE BUFFERS ALSO LESS POWERFUL, ETC.

/	WHILE NOT ADDRESSED IN ANY VERSION OF BIGCON OR CONVRT IMPLEMENTED AS OF THIS
/	WRITING, P?S/8 OVERALL DESIGN ALSO ADDRESSES THE PROBLEM OF ADDRESSING MORE
/	DISK SPACE.  FUTURE VERSIONS OF P?S/8 WILL INCLUDE THE OPTIONAL P?S/8 SHELL
/	OVERLAY.  IN THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT, POWERFUL NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS WILL BE USED
/	TO ADDRESS DEVICES AS LARGE AS 2048 TIMES AS THE LARGEST OS/8 DEVICE ON ANY
/	ONE LOGICAL UNIT OF THE HANDLER.  [NOTE: PRESENT P?S/8 DESIGN ALREADY INCLUDES
/	THE FOUNDATION AND FRAMEWORK FOR THESE HANDLERS; TO DATE ONLY THE P?S/8 BLKCPY
/	UTILITY MAKES USE OF THE NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS.]  AN OS/8 HANDLER CAN ADDRESS AS
/	MUCH AS 1 MW OF STORAGE.  A P?S/8 NON-SYSTEM HANDLER CAN ADDRESS 2 GW OF
/	STORAGE PER LOGICAL DEVICE UNIT FOR A TOTAL ADDRESSIBILITY OF UP TO 16 GW PER
/	HANDLER. [NOTE: MANY MODERN PERIPHERALS ARE INTERNALLY ADDRESSING 8-BIT BYTES.
/	WHEN USED FOR PDP-8 STORAGE PURPOSES, IT IS COMMON TO CONSIDER THE BYTE SIZE
/	AS 6 BITS.  ASSUMING THIS IS THE CASE, A SINGLE P?S/8 NON-SYSTEM DEVICE
/	HANDLER CAN ADDRESS 32 GB OF STORAGE.  ASSUMING THAT THE UNDERLYING STORAGE IS
/	ACTUALLY A MAPPED PORTION OF A STILL-LARGER HARD DISK, IT IS TOTALLY
/	REASONABLE TO EXPECT THE SCOPE OF A P?S/8 SYSTEM TO ADDRESS PERHAPS IN EXCESS
/	OF 2.0 TB TOTAL STORAGE.]

/	THUS, IT IS EXPECTED AN EARLY SHELL UTILITY WILL BE A CONVERSION PROGRAM THAT
/	HAS NO RESTRICTIONS WHATSOEVER IN TERMS OF CONVERTING OS/8 SOURCE CODE TO A
/	P?S/8 SHELL ENVIRONMENT SUITABLY CONFIGURED.  FUTURE VERSIONS COULD INCLUDE
/	OS/8 DIRECTORY SUPPORT FOR AN EVEN MORE ELEGANT APPROACH.  [NOTE: P?S/8 SHELL
/	IS EXPECTED TO BE HARDLY USEFUL ON 4K MACHINES.  THE TARGET SYSTEM IS 16K OR
/	MORE.  HOWEVER, EVERY MEMORY SIZE WILL ALWAYS ATTEMPT TO ACCOMPLISH ANY 
/	OPERATION; APPROPRIATE GRACEFUL ERROR MESSAGES WILL BE ISSUED IF AN OPERATION
/	CANNOT BE ACHIEVED, ETC.]
/	THIS PROGRAM WAS WRITTEN AS THE FIRST STOP-GAP MEASURE IN A LONG STREAM OF
/	EVOLVING DESIGN DECISIONS TO TACKLE ELEMENTS OF THIS CLASS OF PROBLEM.  NOT
/	ALL OF THE CONSIDERATIONS WERE KNOWN AT THE TIME OF THE INITIAL WRITING; THIS
/	EVOLVED OVER TIME LEADING TO VARIOUS ADDITIONAL STOP-GAP MEASURES THAT WERE
/	SOMEWHAT IMPROVED OVER THIS STARTING POINT.  AS OF THIS WRITING, THE LARGEST
/	STEP TO COMPLETELY OVERCOME THIS ENTIRE ISSUE IS IN THE PLANNING STAGES OF THE
/	P?S/8 SHELL.  HOWEVER, FOR THE EXPRESS PURPOSE OF ALLOWING THE P?S/8 PAL
/	ASSEMBLER TO BE VIABLY USED FOR THE LARGEST KNOWN PDP-8 PROJECTS, THIS CLUMSY
/	METHOD AND A SOMEWHAT BETTER IMPLEMENTATION FOR LATER P?S/8 SYSTEMS EXISTS
/	[THE REWRITTEN CONVRT PROGRAM; THIS PROGRAM IS BASED ON THE PRECURSOR CONVRT
/	PROGRAM THAT HAS NO CAPABILITY TO ACCESS FILES LARGER THAN TFS FILES.].

/	FOR THE BENEFIT OF EXISTING EARLY P?S/8 SYSTEMS [PRIMARILY ON TC01/08 DECTAPE
/	MEDIA], THIS PROGRAM WILL BE MODIFIED/[PATCHED TO ALLOW IT TO BE SLIGHTLY MORE
/	VIABLE THAN THIS ORIGINAL VERSION RECOVERED BY DISASSEMBLY.  IT IS GENERALLY
/	TOO DANGEROUS TO USE AS IT STANDS UNLESS THE USER IS EXTREMELY SAVVY OF
/	EXACTLY HOW P?S/8 WORKS INTERNALLY AS THE SYSTEM CAN BE EASILY DESTROYED BY
/	MISUSE OF THE COMMAND-LINE OPTIONS AND THE CONSOLE SWITCH REGISTER!  A
/	SOMEWHAT SAFER VERSION CAN BE ACHIEVED WITH SOME WORK TO REPLACE THIS CODE
/	AS ORIGINALLY WRITTEN.  [SOME OF THE INTERNAL CODE IS A BIT TOO COMPLICATED
/	FOR THE FINAL RESULT ACHIEVED.  IN ALL FAIRNESS, THE PRINCIPLE AUTHOR WITH THE
/	INITIALS RS, WHO WILL NOT BE NAMED, WAS NOT VERY SOPHISTICATED AT THE TIME;
/	HE HAD JUST BEEN EXPOSED TO CO-ROUTINES AND HIS ONLY EXAMPLES TO WORK WITH ARE
/	THOSE IN THE PROGRAM THAT ARE USED TO CONVERT P?S/8 FILES TO OS/8 FILES, WHICH
/	IS ESSENTIALLY REDUNDANT TO THE CONVRT PROGRAM OF THE TIME.  AS SUCH, THIS
/	CODE IS A BIT "ROUGH" AND NOT UP TO THE ADDITIONAL ELEGANCE OF MOST P?S/8
/	PROGRAMS.  EVEN THE PART I [CJL] WROTE NEEDED SOME IMPROVEMENT WHICH CAN BE
/	FOUND IN THE LATER IMPLEMENTATIONS OF CONVRT THAT SUPERSEDED BOTH THIS BIGCON
/	PROGRAM AND THE COMPANION CONVRT PROGRAM FROM THE SAME ERA, ETC.

/	A PDP-8 SOURCE CODE HISTORIAN WILL APPRECIATE THIS SERIES OF PROGRAMS
/	COMPARING THE VARIOUS VERSIONS AS THEY CONTINUE TO EVOLVE.

